IS byRef THE WRAPPER-ADDRESS MECHANISM, AND WOULD WE DESIGN IT ON PURPOSE

⚠ PARKED BY RULING. Tony wants a ruling before any work here. Nothing in this
file is a recommendation and no repair is proposed.

THE QUESTION, in Tony's words (2026-08-24)

    byRef may be an attempt to express wrapper-address -- hand me the local
    itself, not what it contains. The language auto-derefs; wrapper-identity is
    otherwise inexpressible.

CROSS-REFERENCE, AND NOBODY SHOULD RE-DERIVE THIS: commit 23df1b0, runOP must
not unwrap a handle. The iterator fight was plausibly the same fight wearing a
different name. If it was, then the mechanism has been rediscovered at least
twice under two names and never designed once.

WHAT IS STRUCTURAL AND CITABLE -- pointed at, not inferred

  byRef is written in EXACTLY ONE PLACE in the operator layer: opSetFlag's
  case 31 (Instruct.rtn:1145, `target.byRef = true`), reached from incant as
  `x :. byReF` (incant/setup:305 maps byReF to 31). One other write exists,
  in generateCode (Commands.rtn:417). It is READ by opAssign
  (Instruct.rtn:104, `if argument.byRef group = argument`) -- so a byRef
  argument makes a subsequent `=` reference instead of copy, which is
  CLAUDE.md bear-trap 3's sticky behaviour, at its source.

  isPointer is a DIFFERENT flag with the same smell. It is SET by the `&`
  modifier in processFlags (GroupRules.mm:8571) and by the `=*` unary op
  (GroupRules.mm:9829). It is READ in exactly one role: runOP's unwrap
  guards -- GroupRules.mm:12468, 12470, 12740, 12755 -- each of the form

      if isGROUP(target) && !isPointer(target) && !isIterator(target)
          target = target.group

  ⚠ SO isPointer ALREADY IS A "DO NOT AUTO-DEREF THIS HANDLE" MARK, and it
  already sits in the same test as isIterator, which is the other thing that
  must not be unwrapped. That is the structural answer to half the question:
  the wrapper-address mechanism EXISTS, it is called isPointer, it is spelled
  `&`, and it is enforced in one place.

AND THE TWO OPERATORS, since they are what raised the question

  opRebind, the `<-`   (Instruct.rtn:1022-1026)
      if argument target.group = argument;
      -- its own comment: "with NO byRef stamp (unlike :=) and no content copy
      (unlike =)". Sets no flags at all.

  opSetGroup, the `:=` (Instruct.rtn:1179-1186)
      target.gGroup = argument; target.isGROUP = true; target.isInitialized = true;
      -- also no byRef stamp, despite the comment above claiming otherwise.

  ⚠ THE COMMENT AND THE CODE DISAGREE and the code wins on inspection: neither
  operator stamps byRef. The measurable difference is isGROUP, which is exactly
  the flag runOP's unwrap tests -- so `:=` produces a handle the language
  auto-derefs and `<-` produces one it does not. Recorded as a disagreement,
  not resolved here.

WHAT IS NOT KNOWN

Whether byRef and isPointer are two attempts at one idea, or two ideas that
merely rhyme. Whether the iterator exemption (isIterator in those same four
guards) is a third. Nothing here measures intent, and intent is the architect's
to state.

⚠ AND A LIMIT THIS FILE CANNOT ARGUE ITS WAY PAST: neither flag is readable
from incant. opDot resolves about forty numbered accessor cases and byRef and
isPointer are not among them, so an incantation can demonstrate the EFFECTS
(rows 1 and 2 above) and can never print the CAUSE. Any future measurement here
either adds those read cases or runs under a debugger at the anchor.

